  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-04",
    "title": "The Not-So-Hidden Galaxy, IC 342",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  “I see” IC 342, the Hidden Galaxy!  Today’s image  peers through the  plane of the Milky Way , through all its gas, dust and stars, to unveil this  spiral galaxy .  IC 342  (or  Caldwell 5 ) is one of the brightest members of the IC 342/ Maffei  group of galaxies. Located about 10 million  light years  away, this group is quite close to the  Local Group  that includes our Milky Way. Inaccurate accounting of the crowded and dusty Milky Way foreground has led to misunderstandings in IC 342’s history, including the theory that the galaxy was  ejected from the Local Group . IC 342 has its own dust intermittently blocking the  glow of hydrogen gas , indicating the presence of bright, young stars.  Magnetic field maps  of IC 342 reveal that magnetic fields may contribute substantially to one of the biggest galactic puzzles:  how do spiral arms form ?   Tomorrow's picture:  open space",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/ic342.png?w=4144&h=2822&fit=clip&crop=faces,focalpoint",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-4-the-not-so-hidden-galaxy-ic-342/",
    "credit": " Artem Mironov ",
    "alt": "The face of a spiral galaxy fills the image. The galaxy has a bright yellow-ish center. A handful of large arms spiral outward from the center. Bubbles of red glowing gas and tendrils of dark dust trace the spiral arms. Foreground stars and background galaxies are sprinkled across the image.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-04T08:35:06.569028+00:00"
